.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt SSL_NEW_DOMAIN 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm SSL_new_domain ,
.Nm SSL_is_domain ,
.Nm SSL_get0_domain
.Nd SSL object interface for managing QUIC event domains
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/ssl.h
.Ft SSL *
.Fo SSL_new_domain
.Fa "SSL_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "uint64_t flags"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo SSL_is_domain
.Fa "SSL *ssl"
.Fc
.Ft SSL *
.Fo SSL_get0_domain
.Fa "SSL *ssl"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn SSL_new_domain
function creates a new QUIC event domain, represented as an SSL object.
This is known as a QUIC domain SSL object (QDSO). The concept of a QUIC
event domain is discussed in detail in
.Xr openssl-quic-concurrency 7 .
.Pp
The
.Fa flags
argument to
.Fn SSL_new_domain
specifies a set of domain flags.
If the
.Fa flags
argument to
.Fn SSL_new_domain
does not specify one of the flags
.Sy SSL_DOMAIN_FLAG_SINGLE_THREAD ,
.Sy SSL_DOMAIN_FLAG_MULTI_THREAD
or
.Sy SSL_DOMAIN_FLAG_THREAD_ASSISTED ,
the domain flags configured on the
.Vt SSL_CTX
are inherited as a default and any other flags in
.Fa flags
are added to the set of inherited flags.
Otherwise, the domain flags in
.Fa flags
are used.
See
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_domain_flags 3
for details of the available domain flags and how they can be configured
on a
.Vt SSL_CTX .
.Pp
A QUIC domain SSL object can be managed in the same way as any other SSL
object, in that it can be refcounted and freed normally.
A QUIC domain SSL object is the parent of a number of child objects such
as QUIC listener SSL objects.
Once a QUIC domain SSL object has been created, a listener can be
created under it using
.Xr SSL_new_listener_from 3 .
.Pp
.Fn SSL_is_domain
returns 1 if a SSL object is a QUIC domain SSL object.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_get0_domain
obtains a pointer to the QUIC domain SSL object in a SSL object
hierarchy (if any).
.Pp
All SSL objects in a QUIC event domain use the same domain flags, and
the domain flags for a QUIC domain cannot be changed after construction.
.Ss Supported Operations
A QUIC domain SSL object exists to contain other QUIC SSL objects and
provide unified event handling.
As such, it supports only the following operations:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It
Standard reference counting and free operations, such as
.Xr SSL_up_ref 3
and
.Xr SSL_free 3 ;
.It
Event processing and polling enablement APIs such as
.Xr SSL_handle_events 3 ,
and
.Xr SSL_get_event_timeout 3 .
.It
Creating listeners under the domain using
.Xr SSL_new_listener_from 3 .
.El
.Pp
The basic workflow of using a domain object is as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It
Create a new domain object using
.Fn SSL_new_domain
using a
.Vt SSL_CTX
which uses a supported
.Sy SSL_METHOD
(such as
.Xr OSSL_QUIC_server_method 3 Ns );
.It
Create listeners under the domain using
.Xr SSL_new_listener_from 3 .
.El
.Pp
Refer to
.Xr SSL_new_listener_from 3
for details on using listeners.
.Pp
Currently, domain SSL objects are only supported for QUIC usage via any
QUIC
.Sy SSL_METHOD .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn SSL_new_domain
returns a new domain SSL object or NULL on failure.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_is_domain
returns 0 or 1 depending on the type of the SSL object on which it is
called.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_get0_domain
returns an SSL object pointer (potentially to the same object on which
it is called) or NULL.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr SSL_new_listener_from 3
.Xr SSL_handle_events 3 ,
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_domain_flags 3 ,
.Xr openssl-quic-concurrency 7
.Sh HISTORY
These functions were added in OpenSSL 3.5.
